From 65147e3e64644a27876379b8d3dab3ee9a56dc1b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ayiejosh <132535860+ayiejosh@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 07:12:46 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 113/269] gallium/pvr: buffer objects - create, CPU map, unmap,
 close

First real capability beyond enumeration: the driver can now get memory from the
kernel and read and write it, through the same UAPI the Vulkan driver uses
(DRM_IOCTL_PVR_CREATE_BO, _GET_BO_MMAP_OFFSET, GEM_CLOSE). The sequences follow
src/imagination/vulkan/winsys/powervr/pvr_drm_bo.c, which is the working
reference for this interface.

Deliberately four functions, not a winsys abstraction: one implementation, one
caller. GPU address-space mapping needs the heap query and a VM context and is a
separate step - none of these four require either.

Two things worth recording, both read out of the kernel:

* The kernel does NOT round a size up. pvr_drv.c rejects a size that is not a
  whole number of device pages with -EINVAL; the "will be rounded up" prose in
  pvr_gem.c is unreachable through the ioctl because the alignment check fires
  first. So the driver rounds, and the probe asks for 1000 bytes to exercise it.

* DRM_PVR_BO_CPU_CACHED is deliberately NOT used. Mesa's synced header defines
  bit 3, but the DEPLOYED powervr module masks flags to bits 0..2 and returns
  -EINVAL for it - indistinguishable from a size error. The header is ahead of
  the loaded module. Passing it would send you hunting the wrong bug. The default
  write-combined mapping is the coherent one here anyway: WC reads are uncached,
  so the CPU sees GPU writes, and this tree has no userspace cache-maintenance
  path at all.

Runnable check, added to pvr-probe: allocate two buffers (one unaligned size, to
exercise the round-up), map both, confirm a new object arrives zeroed, fill them
with different patterns, verify each reads back only its own bytes, then unmap and
close. Two live mappings with different patterns is what catches confused mmap
offsets - which would otherwise be a silently wrong buffer much later.

Verified on the real open kernel module, not just the shim (the shim does not
implement these ioctls faithfully - it ignores size validation):

  bo: created handles 1 (4096 bytes, asked 1000) and 2 (65536 bytes)
  bo: PASS (create, map, read back, unmap, close)
  --- ALL PASS     exit 0

The check also caught a bug on its first run - in the check itself: it indexed
one buffer's mapping with the other's larger size, reading past the end. Fixed to
verify each mapping over its own length, which still detects aliasing.
